/* data/detail-c/phy-11-01.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/phy-11-01.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-11-01"] = {"phy-11-01-m01":{"idea":"A vector needs both a size and a direction. A scalar needs only a size.","steps":["Displacement is the straight-line change of position, from the start point to the end point.","It has a size (how far) and a direction (which way), so it is a vector.","Speed, distance and temperature have a size only."],"others":"Speed tells only how fast, not which way. Distance is the length of the path and has no direction. Temperature is a plain number, so it is a scalar in every case.","trap":"Distance and displacement sound alike, but only displacement has a direction.","answer":"displacement, which has a magnitude and a direction"},"phy-11-01-m02":{"idea":"A scalar is fully described by a number and a unit. No direction is needed.","steps":["Mass says how much matter a body has. You never say 'a mass towards the north'.","So mass is a scalar.","Force, velocity and acceleration all act in some direction, so they are vectors."],"others":"Velocity, acceleration and force each need a direction as well as a size. That makes all three vectors.","trap":"Quick check: can you say 'towards the east' for it? If yes, it is a vector.","answer":"mass"},"phy-11-01-m03":{"idea":"A unit vector is used only to show a direction. Its size is fixed at 1.","steps":["A unit vector is made by dividing a vector by its own size.","That gives a size of exactly 1.","It has no unit and no dimension. Its only job is to point the way."],"others":"A vector of magnitude 0 is the null vector, not a unit vector. A unit vector has no unit of metre or any other unit. A vector along the x axis with any size is not a unit vector.","trap":"The word 'unit' here means 'one', not a measuring unit like metre.","answer":"has a magnitude of exactly 1, and it shows a direction only"},"phy-11-01-m04":{"idea":"The resultant of two vectors is largest when they point the same way.","steps":["The resultant size is R = √(P² + Q² + 2PQ cos θ), where θ is the angle between P and Q.","cos θ is largest (equal to 1) at θ = 0°.","Then R = √((P + Q)²) = P + Q."],"others":"At 90° the resultant is √(P² + Q²), which is smaller than P + Q. At 180° the resultant is the smallest, not the largest. At 45° the size is less than P + Q, since cos 45° is below 1.","trap":"Adding sizes directly (P + Q) works only when the vectors are in the same direction.","answer":"0°, and the magnitude is P + Q"},"phy-11-01-m05":{"idea":"The resultant is smallest when the two vectors point in opposite directions.","steps":["R = √(P² + Q² + 2PQ cos θ).","The smallest value of cos θ is −1, at θ = 180°.","Then R = √((P − Q)²) = P − Q, since P is greater than Q."],"others":"P + Q at 180° is wrong, since opposite vectors subtract. At 90° the size is √(P² + Q²), not P − Q. At 0° the vectors add up to P + Q, which is the largest value.","trap":"Opposite vectors partly cancel, so the sum is smaller.","answer":"180°, and the magnitude is P − Q"},"phy-11-01-m06":{"idea":"Two forces at right angles combine by Pythagoras' theorem.","steps":["R = √(F₁² + F₂²) when the angle is 90°.","R = √(6² + 8²) = √(36 + 64).","R = √100 = 10 N."],"others":"14 N is the plain sum, which is right only for forces in the same direction. 2 N is the plain difference, right only for opposite forces. 7 N is just the average and has no meaning here.","trap":"6-8-10 is a well known right triangle. It is a fast check.","answer":"10 N"},"phy-11-01-m07":{"idea":"In the triangle law, two vectors are placed head to tail. The resultant closes the triangle.","steps":["Draw the first vector. Draw the second vector from the tip of the first.","The two sides are taken in order, one after the other.","The third side, drawn from the start of the first to the tip of the second, is the resultant. It runs in the opposite order."],"others":"The longest side is not always the resultant. The third side taken in the same order would give a closed loop with zero sum. The sum of the two lengths is true only when the vectors are parallel.","trap":"If all three sides are taken in the same order, the vector sum is zero.","answer":"the third side, taken in the opposite order"},"phy-11-01-m08":{"idea":"The size of a sum of two vectors is never more than the sum of their sizes.","steps":["The triangle inequality says |A + B| ≤ |A| + |B|.","Equality holds only when the two vectors point the same way, so they add fully.","Otherwise some part cancels, so the sum of sizes is the larger."],"others":"It is not always less, because for the same direction the two are equal. It is not always equal, because opposing vectors cancel. The reverse inequality is wrong, since the sum of sizes is the larger or equal value.","trap":"Think of two walks: a bent path is longer than the straight line.","answer":"greater than or equal to it, with equality when they point the same way"},"phy-11-01-m09":{"idea":"A component is the part of a vector along one axis. It is found with sin or cos of the angle.","steps":["The angle θ is measured from the x axis.","Along x: R cos θ (the side next to the angle).","Along y: R sin θ (the side opposite the angle)."],"others":"R cos θ is the x component, not the y component. R tan θ and R/sin θ do not come from the right triangle of components.","trap":"Check at θ = 90°: the vector points along y, so the y component must be R. sin 90° = 1 gives that.","answer":"R sin θ"},"phy-11-01-m10":{"idea":"When the angle is given with the y axis, the roles of sin and cos swap.","steps":["The angle is 30° from the y axis, so the x component is the side opposite to this angle.","x component = 10 sin 30°.","= 10 × 0.5 = 5."],"others":"8.66 is 10 cos 30°, which is the y component. 10 would be the whole vector. 17.3 is larger than the vector itself, which a component can never be.","trap":"A component can never be larger than the vector.","answer":"5"},"phy-11-01-m11":{"idea":"The dot product of two vectors gives a scalar, which depends on cos of the angle.","steps":["The rule is P·Q = P Q cos θ.","The result is a plain number with no direction, so it is a scalar.","The sin θ form belongs to the cross product."],"others":"P Q sin θ is the size of the cross product. The dot product is not a vector. The cross product is a vector, but its size is not a scalar product.","trap":"Dot gives a scalar and uses cos. Cross gives a vector and uses sin.","answer":"P Q cos θ, which is a scalar"},"phy-11-01-m12":{"idea":"A dot product is zero when cos θ is zero, or when a vector is zero.","steps":["P·Q = P Q cos θ.","For non-zero P and Q, this is zero only if cos θ = 0.","So θ = 90°: the vectors are perpendicular."],"others":"Equal sizes tell nothing about the angle. Opposite directions give cos 180° = −1, so the product is −PQ. Parallel vectors give cos 0° = 1, so the product is PQ.","trap":"Zero dot product means perpendicular, not parallel.","answer":"perpendicular to each other"},"phy-11-01-m13":{"idea":"The cross product has a size that depends on sin of the angle between the vectors.","steps":["The size is |P × Q| = P Q sin θ.","It is the area of the parallelogram made by P and Q.","The direction is given by the right-hand rule, at right angles to both."],"others":"P Q cos θ is the dot product. P Q alone would be right only at θ = 90°. P Q tan θ does not come from either product.","trap":"Mixing up sin (cross) and cos (dot) is the usual slip.","answer":"P Q sin θ"},"phy-11-01-m14":{"idea":"Parallel vectors enclose no area, so their cross product is zero.","steps":["|P × Q| = P Q sin θ.","For parallel vectors θ = 0°, and sin 0° = 0.","So the product is the zero vector."],"others":"A unit vector is not given by this product. sin 0° is 0, not 1, so the product is not at its greatest value. The cross product is a vector, so it cannot be a scalar PQ.","trap":"The cross product is greatest at 90°, and zero at 0° and 180°.","answer":"the zero vector, as sin 0° = 0"},"phy-11-01-m15":{"idea":"The cross product changes sign when the order of the vectors is swapped.","steps":["P × Q has the size P Q sin θ and a direction by the right-hand rule.","Swapping the order turns the rotation the other way.","So the direction reverses: P × Q = −(Q × P)."],"others":"The two products are equal only if both are zero, not for perpendicular vectors. Squaring a vector product makes no sense here. The cross product is not commutative, so the two are not equal in general.","trap":"Dot product: order does not matter. Cross product: order matters.","answer":"P × Q = −(Q × P)"},"phy-11-01-m16":{"idea":"The unit vectors i, j and k follow the right-hand rule: i × j = k.","steps":["i points along x and j points along y. They are perpendicular, so the size is 1 × 1 × sin 90° = 1.","By the right-hand rule, the direction is along +z.","So i × j = k."],"others":"The plain number 1 is wrong, since a cross product is a vector. The zero result is for i × i, not i × j. −k is the result of j × i, the reverse order.","trap":"i × j = k, j × k = i, k × i = j. The reverse order gives a minus sign.","answer":"k"},"phy-11-01-m17":{"idea":"Torque depends on the angle between r and F. Along the same line it is zero.","steps":["τ = r × F, and its size is r F sin θ.","If r and F are along the same line, θ = 0° (or 180°).","So sin θ = 0 and the torque is zero. A force through the axis cannot turn the body."],"others":"The value rF is for θ = 90°, not 0°. The torque is defined, and its value is zero. It is the least, not the maximum, when the vectors are on one line.","trap":"To turn a door you push at right angles, not along the door.","answer":"zero, since the cross product of parallel vectors is zero"},"phy-11-01-m18":{"idea":"Work is the part of the force along the displacement. It is a scalar.","steps":["Work W = F·d = F d cos θ, where θ is the angle between F and d.","A dot product gives a scalar.","So work has a size only, with no direction."],"others":"A cross product F × d is a vector and is not work. The dot product is not a vector, so calling work a vector is wrong.","trap":"Work and energy are scalars, even though force and displacement are vectors.","answer":"dot product F·d, which is a scalar"},"phy-11-01-m19":{"idea":"A derivative measures how fast one quantity changes with another.","steps":["dy/dx is the limit of Δy/Δx when Δx becomes very small.","On the graph, this is the slope of the tangent at that point.","For example, the slope of an x–t graph is the velocity."],"others":"The area under the graph is the integral, not the derivative. The function value is just y. The length of the graph is a different quantity found by another formula.","trap":"Derivative gives slope. Integral gives area.","answer":"the slope of the tangent to the graph at that point"},"phy-11-01-m20":{"idea":"The power rule: bring the power down, then reduce the power by one.","steps":["For y = xⁿ, dy/dx = n xⁿ⁻¹.","Example: x³ gives 3x².","So the answer is n xⁿ⁻¹."],"others":"xⁿ⁻¹ forgets the factor n. xⁿ/n is not a derivative at all. n xⁿ⁺¹ raises the power instead of lowering it.","trap":"Derivative lowers the power. Integral raises it.","answer":"n xⁿ⁻¹"},"phy-11-01-m21":{"idea":"The product rule: differentiate one function at a time, keeping the other fixed.","steps":["For y = f₁ f₂, take f₁ times the derivative of f₂.","Then add f₂ times the derivative of f₁.","So dy/dx = f₁ f₂′ + f₂ f₁′."],"others":"f₁′ f₂′ multiplies the two derivatives, which is wrong. The ratio form is not a rule for any product. f₁ f₂ + f₁′ f₂′ mixes functions with derivatives and has no basis.","trap":"Check with f₁ = f₂ = x: the product is x², and the derivative is 2x. The rule gives x + x = 2x.","answer":"f₁ f₂′ + f₂ f₁′"},"phy-11-01-m22":{"idea":"The chain rule: differentiate the outside, then multiply by the derivative of the inside.","steps":["Let u = 3x. Then y = sin u.","dy/dx = cos u × du/dx = cos(3x) × 3.","So the derivative is 3 cos(3x)."],"others":"cos(3x) forgets the factor 3 from the inside. −3 cos(3x) has a wrong sign, since sin gives +cos. 3 sin(3x) did not change sin into cos.","trap":"Do not forget the derivative of the inside function.","answer":"3 cos(3x)"},"phy-11-01-m23":{"idea":"The definite integral gives the area under the curve between two limits.","steps":["∫x² dx = x³/3.","Put in the limits: (3³/3) − (0³/3).","= 27/3 − 0 = 9."],"others":"27 is x³ without the division by 3. 3 is not related to any step of the working. 18 comes from a wrong factor of 2 after integrating.","trap":"After integrating x², the power becomes 3 and you divide by 3.","answer":"9"},"phy-11-01-m24":{"idea":"The distance is the area under the v–t graph, so we integrate the velocity.","steps":["Distance = ∫v dt = ∫3t² dt from 0 to 2.","∫3t² dt = t³.","At the limits: 2³ − 0³ = 8 m."],"others":"4 m comes from using t² instead of t³. 12 m is the velocity at t = 2 s (3 × 4), which is not a distance. 6 m comes from a wrong factor after integrating.","trap":"Integration of velocity gives distance. Putting t = 2 in v gives speed, not distance.","answer":"8 m"},"phy-11-01-n01":{"idea":"Two forces at right angles combine by Pythagoras' theorem.","steps":["R = √(F₁² + F₂²) at 90°.","R = √(3² + 4²) = √(9 + 16).","R = √25 = 5.0 N."],"trap":"3-4-5 is a standard right triangle, so the answer is easy to check.","answer":"5.0 N"},"phy-11-01-n02":{"idea":"When two forces are at an angle θ, use the law of cosines for the resultant.","steps":["R = √(P² + Q² + 2PQ cos θ).","R² = 10² + 6² + 2 × 10 × 6 × cos 60° = 100 + 36 + 60.","R² = 196, so R = 14.00 N."],"trap":"The result lies between 10 − 6 = 4 N and 10 + 6 = 16 N. 14 N fits.","answer":"14.00 N"},"phy-11-01-n03":{"idea":"The direction of a resultant is found from its components along and across the first force.","steps":["tan α = Q sin θ / (P + Q cos θ), with P = 10 N, Q = 6 N and θ = 60°.","Q sin θ = 6 × 0.866 = 5.196. P + Q cos θ = 10 + 3 = 13.","tan α = 5.196/13 = 0.3997, so α = 21.79°."],"trap":"The resultant must lie between the two forces, nearer to the larger one (10 N). 21.79° is less than 60°, so this fits.","answer":"21.79°"},"phy-11-01-n04":{"idea":"The dot product of two vectors in component form is the sum of the products of matching components.","steps":["A·B = AₓBₓ + AᵧBᵧ + A_zB_z.","= (2)(1) + (3)(2) + (1)(−1).","= 2 + 6 − 1 = 7."],"trap":"The dot product is a scalar, so there are no i, j or k in the answer.","answer":"7"},"phy-11-01-n05":{"idea":"The cross product in component form can be found with a determinant. Then take its size.","steps":["A × B = (2·1 − 3·(−1)) i + (3·2 − 1·1) j + (1·(−1) − 2·2) k.","= 5i + 5j − 5k.","Size = √(25 + 25 + 25) = √75 = 5√3 = 8.66."],"trap":"Check: the result must be at right angles to A. Its dot product with A is 5 + 10 − 15 = 0.","answer":"8.66"},"phy-11-01-n06":{"idea":"The angle between two vectors comes from the dot product: A·B = AB cos θ.","steps":["A·B = (1)(0) + (1)(1) + (0)(1) = 1.","A = √2 and B = √2, so AB = 2.","cos θ = 1/2, so θ = 60.0°."],"trap":"cos θ must be between −1 and 1. 0.5 is fine.","answer":"60.0°"},"phy-11-01-n07":{"idea":"The cross product A × B gives the area of the parallelogram. A triangle is half of it.","steps":["Parallelogram area = |A × B| = 3 × 4 × sin 90° = 12 m².","A triangle on the same two sides is half the parallelogram.","Area = 12/2 = 6.0 m²."],"trap":"Do not stop at 12 m²: the question asks for the triangle.","answer":"6.0 m²"},"phy-11-01-n08":{"idea":"Use the product rule, since y is the product of x³ and sin x.","steps":["dy/dx = x³ cos x + 3x² sin x.","At x = π/2: cos = 0 and sin = 1, so dy/dx = 3(π/2)².","= 3 × 2.4674 = 7.402."],"trap":"Use radians for π/2. The slope is positive and fairly steep, which fits the curve.","answer":"7.402"},"phy-11-01-n09":{"idea":"Use the chain rule: differentiate the power first, then the inside.","steps":["Let u = 3x² + 2. Then y = u⁴.","dy/dx = 4u³ × du/dx = 4(3x² + 2)³ × 6x.","At x = 1: u = 5, so dy/dx = 4 × 125 × 6 = 3000."],"trap":"The factor 6x (inside derivative) is the part that is often forgotten.","answer":"3000"},"phy-11-01-n10":{"idea":"Use the quotient rule: (bottom × derivative of top − top × derivative of bottom) / bottom².","steps":["dy/dx = [(x² + 1)(1) − x(2x)] / (x² + 1)² = (1 − x²)/(x² + 1)².","At x = 2: (1 − 4)/(5)² = −3/25.","= −0.12."],"trap":"The curve y = x/(x² + 1) falls after x = 1, so a negative slope at x = 2 is right.","answer":"-0.12"},"phy-11-01-n11":{"idea":"A definite integral adds up the area under the line between the two limits.","steps":["∫(2x + 1) dx = x² + x.","At x = 3: 9 + 3 = 12. At x = 0: 0.","Value = 12 − 0 = 12.0."],"trap":"Check with a trapezium: heights 1 and 7, width 3. Area = (1 + 7)/2 × 3 = 12.","answer":"12.0"},"phy-11-01-n12":{"idea":"A unit vector is the vector divided by its own size.","steps":["The size of 3i + 4j is √(3² + 4²) = 5.","The unit vector is (3i + 4j)/5 = 0.6i + 0.8j.","Its x component is 0.60."],"trap":"The size of the unit vector must be 1: 0.6² + 0.8² = 1.","answer":"0.60"},"phy-11-01-n13":{"idea":"Work is the dot product of force and displacement.","steps":["W = F·d = (2)(1) + (3)(−1) + (4)(2)","= 2 − 3 + 8.","W = 7 J."],"trap":"Work is a scalar. The negative term means that part of the force opposes that part of the motion.","answer":"7 J"}};
